# Dependencies And Parallel Work

A Flow graph gets dependencies from result references and explicit `after`
entries. Source order does not create a dependency.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.6"}])
```

## Define A Diamond

```elixir
defmodule FlowDependencies.Echo do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_dependencies_echo"

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule FlowDependencies.Diamond do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_dependencies_diamond"

  flow do
    step "left",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{side: :left, value: input(:value)}

    step "right",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{side: :right, value: input(:value)}

    step "join",
      action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
      params: %{
        left: result("left", :value),
        right: result("right", :value)
      }

    output(%{joined: result("join")})
  end
end
```

`left` and `right` are independent. `join` depends on both because its
parameter expression reads both results.

```elixir
{:ok, dependencies} =
  FlowDependencies.Diamond.flow()
  |> Jido.Flow.dependencies()

dependencies
```

## Use After For Control Only

`after:` orders work without copying predecessor output into the next Action.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
step "publish",
  action: FlowDependencies.Echo,
  params: %{status: :published},
  after: ["audit", "approve"]
```

Use a result reference when the Action needs data. Use `after` only when the
dependency is an author control decision.

Jido keeps the three forms separate:

```elixir
%{
  after: ["audit"],
  references: ["render"],
  effective: ["audit", "render"]
}
```

## Execute A Ready Wave

```elixir
{:ok, execution} =
  Jido.Exec.start(FlowDependencies.Diamond, %{value: 4})

Jido.Exec.ready(execution)
```

The ready values are native `Runic.Workflow.Runnable` values. Independent
authored nodes can be in the same ready set. Run one current set with
`wave/1`.

```elixir
{:ok, _runnables, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)
{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.continue(execution)
{:ok, %{joined: %{left: 4, right: 4}}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

Ready work runs concurrently when `max_concurrency` is greater than `1`.
`max_concurrency` bounds the execution and defaults to `8`. Use `1` for serial
execution. The option does not change the graph.
